chapter Root
  chapter Philosophy
    text
      I believe programming should be more personal. Currently (2026), software
      tools are mostly developed by a few people in a central place and then
      used by many more people. However, you very rarely modify or customize
      your software tools. Even experienced programmers using open-source tools
      rarely look at the source code.
    text
      The current state is practical: It takes a lot of effort to build
      software, so naturally you need to bundle resources to create big
      projects. I like that this necessity to collaborate fosters social
      relationships and gets people to explore problem spaces together. However,
      there is also a place for more personal, customized, specialized, smaller
      software. This vision existed since the early days of computing: Alan Kay
      envisioned computers as "personal dynamic media", where every user feels
      comfortable to create, use, and customize tools. Orchard is my attempt at
      creating a personal computing medium.
  chapter Core
    chapter Nothing
      text
        Nothing is a type with only one instance. You might know it from other
        languages as unit or void. From an information theoretical perspective,
        being given an instance of a Nothing type gives you no information
        whatsoever.
        Nothing is used whenever we don't actually want to provide a useful
        value. Functions that only have side-effects (aka they may crash or hang
        but that's all they do) return Nothing. See the assert function for an
        example.
        Functions without an explicit return type return Nothing by default.
        Empty bodies return Nothing. If you really need a Nothing, you can just
        write curly braces.
      code
        Nothing = struct {}
      code
        ignore[T](value: T) = {}
    chapter Never
      text
        Never is a type with zero instances. If you write a function that
        accepts an argument of Never, it can never be called (otherwise, the
        caller would have a Never instance).
        Why do we need something like this? Some expressions always abort
        control flow, for example `panic("hi")`. These evaluate to the Never
        type. Because Never is never instantiated, it can be assigned to
        anything: `fibonacci(todo())`
      code
        Never = enum {}
    chapter Do
      code
        do[A, B](value: A, doer: fun(A) B) B = doer(value)
    chapter Dynamic
      text
        Dynamic holds an object whose type is not tracked by the type system.
        value.to_dynamic() forgets the static type; dynamic.to_static[T]() treats
        the object as T. Both conversions return the same heap object without
        copying it or adding a wrapper.
        to_static[T]() does not check the object's type. The programmer must
        supply the correct T; using the wrong type is undefined behavior.
      code
        Dynamic = ...
        to_dynamic[T](value: T) Dynamic = ...
        to_static[T](value: Dynamic) T = ...
    chapter Bool
      text
        After the Never type with zero instances and the Nothing type with one
        instance, Norwegian scientists discovered a groundbreaking concept: A
        type with two instances!
      code
        Bool = enum { false, true }
      text
        Please, please, please don't reassign.
      code
        true = Bool.true
      code
        false = Bool.false
      code
        not(a: Bool) Bool = if a false true
      code
        ==(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool = if a b not(b)
        !=(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool = not(a == b)
      code
        implies(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool = if a b true
    chapter AndOr
      text
        The "and" and "or" keywords allow short-circuiting.

        Here are cases where the control flow short-circuits and the
        alternatives don't get evaluated:

        false and panic() | this is false
        true or panic()   | this is true
        some(2) or 3      | this is 2

        In these cases, the control flow doesn't short-circuit and the
        alternatives are evaluated as well:

        false or foo      | this is foo
        true and stuff    | this is stuff
        none[Int]() or 3  | this is 3

        The keywords turn into calls of left.and() or left.or(), which returns a
        ControlFlow. This logic flow determines whether the expression
        short-circuits or not.
      code
        ControlFlow[A, B] = enum {
          short_circuit: A, evaluate_alternative: B }
      text
        Bools are short-circuiting.
      code
        and(bool: Bool) ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing] =
          if bool
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].evaluate_alternative
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].short_circuit(false)
      code
        or(bool: Bool) ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing] =
          if bool
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].short_circuit(true)
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].evaluate_alternative
    chapter Ordering
      text
        Consider implementing the spaceship operator <=>(T, T) Ordering for
        types that can be compared.
      code
        Ordering = enum { less, equal, greater }
      code
        ==(a: Ordering, b: Ordering) Bool =
          a %(less b is less equal b is equal greater b is greater)
        flip(ordering: Ordering) Ordering =
          ordering %(
            less Ordering.greater equal Ordering.equal greater Ordering.less)
        is_less(o: Ordering) Bool = o is less
        is_equal(o: Ordering) Bool = o is equal
        is_greater(o: Ordering) Bool = o is greater
        is_greater_equal(o: Ordering) Bool = not(o is less)
        is_not_equal(o: Ordering) Bool = not(o is equal)
        is_less_equal(o: Ordering) Bool = not(o is greater)
    chapter Byte
      text
        A byte represents a number between 0 and 255, inclusive.

        Because Orchard's heap is word-based, a byte is really just a leaf
        object that takes up two words (header and data). So, for performance,
        you should store byte sequences as strings instead, only constructing
        Byte instances on demand (for example, when iterating over the
        sequence).
      code
        Byte = ...
      text
        Byte arithmetic wraps into the range 0 to 255.
      code
        +(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        -(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        *(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        /(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        mod(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        rem(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        &(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        |(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        xor(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte = ...
        <=>(left: Byte, right: Byte) Ordering = ...
        ==(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = a <=> b is equal
        <(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = a <=> b is less
        >(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = a <=> b is greater
        !=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = not(a <=> b is equal)
        >=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = not(a <=> b is less)
        <=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool = not(a <=> b is greater)
    chapter String
      text
        A String is what you get from string literals such as "blub". There is
        no constraint that strings have to be valid UTF-8 or something like that
        – you can use them for arbitrary byte sequences.
      code
        String = ...
        concat(left: String, right: String) String = ...
        +(left: String, right: String) String = concat(left, right)
    chapter Crashing
      text
        Crashing stops evaluation with a message.
      code
        crash(message: String) Never = ...
        crash() Never = crash("crashed")
      code
        unreachable() Never = crash("unreachable")
      code
        todo() Never = crash("todo")
    chapter Assert
      code
        assert(condition: Bool, message: String) =
          if not(condition) crash(message) {}
        assert(condition: Bool) = assert(condition, "assert failed")
    chapter Maybe
      text
        Represents optional values. Like Option in Rust, but with the cooler
        Haskell name.
      code
        Maybe[A] = enum { some: A, none }
      code
        some[A](value: A) Maybe[A] = Maybe.some(value)
        none[A]() Maybe[A] = Maybe[A].none
      code
        or[A](maybe: Maybe[A]) ControlFlow[A, Nothing] =
          maybe %(
            some(a) ControlFlow[A, Nothing].short_circuit(a)
            none ControlFlow[A, Nothing].evaluate_alternative)
      code
        map[A, B](maybe: Maybe[A], mapper: fun(A) B) Maybe[B] =
          maybe %(some(a) some(mapper(a)) none none[B]())
        flatten[A](maybe: Maybe[Maybe[A]]) Maybe[A] = maybe or none[A]()
        flat_map[A, B](maybe: Maybe[A], mapper: fun(A) Maybe[B]) Maybe[B] =
          maybe.map(mapper).flatten()
        unwrap[A](maybe: Maybe[A], message: String) A = maybe or crash(message)
        unwrap[A](maybe: Maybe[A]) A = maybe.unwrap("called none.unwrap")
    chapter Result
      text
        The result of something that can either work or fail.
      code
        Result[O, E] = enum { ok: O, error: E }
      code
        ok[O, E](o: O) Result[O, E] = Result[O, E].ok(o)
        error[O, E](e: E) Result[O, E] = Result[O, E].error(e)
      code
        or[O, E](result: Result[O, E]) ControlFlow[O, E] =
          result %(
            ok(o) ControlFlow[O, E].short_circuit(o)
            error(e) ControlFlow[O, E].evaluate_alternative(e))
      code
        unwrap[O, E](result: Result[O, E], message: String) O =
          result %(ok(o) o error crash(message))
        unwrap[O, E](result: Result[O, E]) O =
          result %(ok(o) o error(e) crash("called error.unwrap"))
      code
        map[O, E, A](result: Result[O, E], mapper: fun(O) A) Result[A, E] =
          result %(ok(o) ok[A, E](mapper(o)) error(e) error[A, E](e))
        map_error[O, E, A](
          result: Result[O, E], mapper: fun(E) A) Result[O, A] =
          result %(ok(o) ok[O, A](o) error(e) error[O, A](mapper(e)))
    chapter Int
      text
        Ints are 64-bit signed integers. If you write a number such as 42 in the
        code, it's an Int.
      code
        Int = ...
      code
        +(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        -(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        *(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        /(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        mod(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        rem(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        &(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        |(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        xor(left: Int, right: Int) Int = ...
        negate(int: Int) Int = 0 - int
        <=>(left: Int, right: Int) Ordering = ...
        ==(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = a <=> b is equal
        <(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = a <=> b is less
        >(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = a <=> b is greater
        !=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = not(a <=> b is equal)
        >=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = not(a <=> b is less)
        <=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool = not(a <=> b is greater)
        <<(int: Int, by: Int) Int =
          if by == 0 int int * 2 << {by - 1}
        >>(int: Int, by: Int) Int =
          if by == 0 int int / 2 << {by - 1}
        abs(value: Int) Int = if value >= 0 value 0 - value
        max(a: Int, b: Int) Int = if a > b a b
        min(a: Int, b: Int) Int = if a < b a b
        is_even(a: Int) Bool = mod(a, 2) == 0
        is_odd(a: Int) Bool = mod(a, 2) == 1
        pow(base: Int, exponent: Int) Int =
          if exponent == 0 1 base * base.pow(exponent - 1)
        round_up_to_multiple_of(number: Int, factor: Int) Int =
          number + factor - 1 / factor * factor
        round_up_to_power_of(number: Int, base: Int) Int =
          number.round_up_to_power_of_rec(base, 1)
        round_up_to_power_of_rec(number: Int, base: Int, candidate: Int) Int =
          if candidate >= number
            candidate
            number.round_up_to_power_of_rec(base, candidate * base)
      code
        sqrt(number: Int) Int =
          _ = assert(
            number >= 0, "You can't take the square root of a negative number.")
          sqrt_rec(number, 0)
        sqrt_rec(number: Int, candidate: Int) Int =
          next = candidate + 1
          next * next <=> number %(
            less sqrt_rec(number, next) equal next greater candidate)
        log_2(value: Int) Int = if value == 1 0 log_2(value / 2) + 1
      code
        # stuff for bytes
        lower_byte(value: Int) Byte = ...
        to_int(value: Byte) Int = ...
        to_byte(value: Int) Byte =
          _ = assert(value >= 0 and value < 256)
          value.lower_byte()
        <<(byte: Byte, by: Byte) Byte =
          {byte.to_int() << by.to_int()}.lower_byte()
        >>(byte: Byte, by: Byte) Byte =
          {byte.to_int() >> by.to_int()}.lower_byte()
        min(a: Byte, b: Byte) Byte = if a < b a b
        max(a: Byte, b: Byte) Byte = if a > b a b
      text
        format(byte: Byte) String = byte.to_int().format()
      code
        min_int = -9223372036854775808
        max_int = 9223372036854775807
        min_byte = lower_byte(0)
        max_byte = lower_byte(255)
    chapter Float
      text
        Floats are 64-bit IEEE 754 floating point numbers. If you write a number
        such as 1.2 in the code, it's a Float.
      code
        Float = ...
        to_float(value: Int) Float = ...
        to_int(value: Float) Int = ...
        +(left: Float, right: Float) Float = ...
        -(left: Float, right: Float) Float = ...
        *(left: Float, right: Float) Float = ...
        /(left: Float, right: Float) Float = ...
        <=>(left: Float, right: Float) Ordering = ...
        ==(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = ...
        !=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = not(left == right)
        <(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = ...
        >(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = ...
        <=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = ...
        >=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool = ...
        abs(value: Float) Float = if value >= 0.0 value 0.0 - value
        max(a: Float, b: Float) Float = if a > b a b
        min(a: Float, b: Float) Float = if a < b a b
    chapter Range
      text
        Ranges have an inclusive start and an exclusive end.
        All operators have the same precedence and associate from left to right.
      code
        Range[A] = struct { start: A, end: A }
        ..[A](start: A, end: A) Range[A] = Range(start, end)
        ..=(start: Int, end: Int) Range[Int] = start .. {end + 1}
        ..+(start: Int, length: Int) Range[Int] = start .. {start + length}
        ..+=(start: Int, length: Int) Range[Int] = start .. {start + length + 1}
        +-(mid: Int, distance: Int) Range[Int] =
          {mid - distance} .. {mid + distance + 1}
        length(range: Range[Int]) Int = range.end - range.start
        contains(range: Range[Int], value: Int) Bool =
          value >= range.start and value < range.end
        contains(range: Range[Float], value: Float) Bool =
          value >= range.start and value < range.end
        is_empty(range: Range[Int]) Bool = range.start >= range.end
        is_not_empty(range: Range[Int]) Bool = not(range.is_empty())
      code
        Infinity = struct {}
        infinity = Infinity()
      code
        OpenRange[A] = struct { start: A }
        ..[A](start: A, infinity: Infinity) OpenRange[A] = OpenRange(start)
    chapter LinkedList
      code
        LinkedList[A] = enum { empty, more: More[A] }
        More[A] = struct { head: A, tail: LinkedList[A] }
      code
        empty_linked_list[A]() LinkedList[A] = LinkedList[A].empty
        cons[A](head: A, tail: LinkedList[A]) LinkedList[A] =
          LinkedList.more(More(head, tail))
        push[A](list: LinkedList[A], item: A) LinkedList[A] =
          list %(
            empty cons(item, empty_linked_list[A]())
            more(more) cons(more.head, more.tail.push(item)))
        get[A](list: LinkedList[A], index: Int) A =
          list %(
            empty crash("The index is out of bounds.")
            more(more) if index == 0 more.head more.tail.get(index - 1))
    chapter List
      text
        List[A] is an immutable array.
      code
        List[A] = ...
        list[T]() List[T] = ...
        length[T](list: List[T]) Int = ...
        get[T](list: List[T], index: Int) T = ...
        filled_list[T](length: Int, fill: T) List[T] = ...
        push[T](list: List[T], item: T) List[T] = ...
        push_all[T](list: List[T], items: List[T]) List[T] = ...
        slice[T](list: List[T], range: Range[Int]) List[T] = ...
        set[T](list: List[T], index: Int, item: T) List[T] = ...
        reversed[T](list: List[T]) List[T] = ...
        sum(list: List[Int]) Int = ...
      code
        is_empty[A](list: List[A]) Bool = list.length() == 0
        is_not_empty[A](list: List[A]) Bool = not(list.is_empty())
        get_maybe[A](list: List[A], index: Int) Maybe[A] =
          if {0..list.length()}.contains(index) some(list.get(index)) none[A]()
        list[A](a: A) List[A] = list[A]().push(a)
        list[A](a: A, b: A) List[A] = list(a).push(b)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A) List[A] = list(a, b).push(c)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A) List[A] = list(a, b, c).push(d)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A) List[A] = list(a, b, c, d).push(e)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e).push(f)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f).push(g)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g).push(h)
        list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h).push(i)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i).push(j)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j).push(k)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A, l: A
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k).push(l)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l).push(m)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m).push(n)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n).push(o)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o).push(p)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p).push(q)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q).push(r)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r).push(s)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s).push(t)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t)
            .push(u)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A, v: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u)
            .push(v)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A, v: A,
          w: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v)
            .push(w)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A, v: A,
          w: A, x: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(
            a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v, w)
            .push(x)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A, v: A,
          w: A, x: A, y: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(
            a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v, w,
            x)
            .push(y)
        list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A, k: A,
          l: A, m: A, n: A, o: A, p: A, q: A, r: A, s: A, t: A, u: A, v: A,
          w: A, x: A, y: A, z: A,
          ) List[A] =
          list(
            a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v, w,
            x, y)
            .push(z)
      code
        first[A](list: List[A]) A = list.get(0)
        last[A](list: List[A]) A = list.get(list.length() - 1)
        first[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] = list.slice(0..amount)
        last[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] =
          _ = assert(
            amount >= 0 and amount <= list.length(),
            "List amount out of bounds.")
          list.slice({list.length() - amount}..list.length())
        without_first[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] =
          list.slice(amount..list.length())
        without_first[A](list: List[A]) List[A] = list.without_first(1)
        without_last[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] =
          _ = assert(
            amount >= 0 and amount <= list.length(),
            "List amount out of bounds.")
          list.slice(0..{list.length() - amount})
        without_last[A](list: List[A]) List[A] = list.without_last(1)
        pop[A](list: List[A]) List[A] =
          _ = assert(list.is_not_empty(), "Cannot pop an empty list.")
          list.without_last()
        first_maybe[A](list: List[A]) Maybe[A] = list.get_maybe(0)
        last_maybe[A](list: List[A]) Maybe[A] =
          list.get_maybe(list.length() - 1)
        insert[A](list: List[A], index: Int, item: A) List[A] =
          list.first(index).push(item).push_all(list.without_first(index))
        remove[A](list: List[A], range: Range[Int]) List[A] =
          _ = assert(
            0 <= range.start
              and range.start <= range.end
              and range.end <= list.length(),
            "List range out of bounds.")
          list.first(range.start).push_all(list.without_first(range.end))
    chapter StringBytes
      text
        bytes returns the string's UTF-8 bytes; string packs a byte list into
        a String. Packed strings use zero padding, so trailing zero bytes are
        indistinguishable from padding. Embedded zero bytes are retained.
        TODO: Instead of eagerly constructing a giant array, make this return an
        iterator?
      code
        bytes(value: String) List[Byte] = ...
        string(bytes: List[Byte]) String = ...
      code
        char(string: String) Byte =
          bytes = string.bytes()
          _ = assert(bytes.length() == 1, "Expected a single-byte character")
          bytes.get(0)
      code
        parse_int(string: String, radix: Int) Int =
          string.bytes().parse_int(radix)
        parse_int(bytes: List[Byte], radix: Int) Int =
          digit = bytes.get(0).parse_digit()
          if bytes.length() == 1
            digit
            digit * radix.pow(bytes.length() - 1)
              + bytes.without_first(1).parse_int(radix)
        # TODO: throw an error if the string contains a digit above the radix
        parse_digit(char: Byte) Int =
          value = char.to_int()
          if value >= 48 and value <= 57
            value - 48
          if value >= 97 and value <= 102
            value - 97 + 10
          if value >= 65 and value <= 70
            value - 65 + 10
            crash("Invalid digit")
    chapter Tuples
      code
        Tuple2[A, B] = struct { a: A, b: B }
        Tuple3[A, B, C] = struct { a: A, b: B, c: C }
      code
        tuple[A, B](a: A, b: B) Tuple2[A, B] = Tuple2(a, b)
        tuple[A, B, C](a: A, b: B, c: C) Tuple3[A, B, C] = Tuple3(a, b, c)
    chapter Loop
      code
        Loop[S, R] = enum { continue: S, break: R }
        loop[S, R](state: S, step: fun(S) Loop[S, R]) R =
          step(state) %(continue(next) loop(next, step) break(result) result)
        loop[A, B, R](a: A, b: B, step: fun(A, B) Loop[Tuple2[A, B], R]) R =
          loop(tuple(a, b), \ state: Tuple2[A, B] -> step(state.a, state.b))
    chapter Iterators
      text
        Iterators are defined by their behavior (you can get the next item)
        rather than their structure. That's why iterators (Iter for short) just
        contain a function pointer.
      code
        Iter[A] = struct { next: fun() Maybe[Next[A]] }
        Next[A] = struct { item: A, rest: Iter[A] }
      code
        iter_from_fun[A](next: fun() Maybe[Next[A]]) Iter[A] = Iter(next)
      code
        NextState[A, S] = struct { item: A, state: S }
        iter_from_state[A, S](
          state: S, next: fun(S) Maybe[NextState[A, S]]
        ) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            next(state).map(\ stuff: NextState[A, S] ->
              Next(
                item = stuff.item,
                rest = iter_from_state(stuff.state, next))))
      code
        is_empty[A](iter: Iter[A]) Bool = iter.next() is none
      code
        filter_map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) Maybe[B]) Iter[B] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next().flat_map(\ next: Next[A] ->
              mapper(next.item) %(
                none next.rest.filter_map(mapper).next()
                some(b)
                  some(Next(item = b, rest = next.rest.filter_map(mapper))))))
      code
        map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) B) Iter[B] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next().map(\ next: Next[A] ->
              Next(item = mapper(next.item), rest = map(next.rest, mapper))))
      code
        map[A, B, C](iter: Iter[Tuple2[A, B]], mapper: fun(A, B) C) Iter[C] =
          iter.map(\ pair: Tuple2[A, B] -> mapper(pair.a, pair.b))
      code
        somes[A](iter: Iter[Maybe[A]]) Iter[A] =
          iter.filter_map(\ value: Maybe[A] -> value)
      code
        Indexed[A] = struct { index: Int, item: A }
        indexed[A](iter: Iter[A]) Iter[Indexed[A]] = iter.indexed_rec(0)
        indexed_rec[A](iter: Iter[A], start: Int) Iter[Indexed[A]] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next().map(\ next: Next[A] ->
              Next(
                item = Indexed(index = start, item = next.item),
                rest = next.rest.indexed_rec(start + 1))))
      code
        map_indexed[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(Int, A) B) Iter[B] =
          iter.indexed().map(\ indexed: Indexed[A] ->
            mapper(indexed.index, indexed.item))
      code
        filter[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next() %(
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next)
                if checker(next.item)
                  some(Next(item = next.item, rest = next.rest.filter(checker)))
                  next.rest.filter(checker).next()))
      code
        fold[A, S](iter: Iter[A], state: S, folder: fun(S, A) S) S =
          iter.next() %(
            none state
            some(next) next.rest.fold(folder(state, next.item), folder))
      code
        fold[Item, A, B](
          iter: Iter[Item], a: A, b: B,
          folder: fun(A, B, Item) Tuple2[A, B],
        ) Tuple2[A, B] =
          iter.fold(tuple(a, b), \ state: Tuple2[A, B], item: Item ->
            folder(state.a, state.b, item))
        fold[Item, A, B, C](
          iter: Iter[Item], a: A, b: B, c: C,
          folder: fun(A, B, C, Item) Tuple3[A, B, C],
        ) Tuple3[A, B, C] =
          iter.fold(tuple(a, b, c), \ state: Tuple3[A, B, C], item: Item ->
            folder(state.a, state.b, state.c, item))
      code
        reduce[A](iter: Iter[A], reducer: fun(A, A) A) Maybe[A] =
          iter.next() %(
            none none[A]()
            some(next) some(next.rest.fold(next.item, reducer)))
      code
        all[A](iter: Iter[A], check: fun(A) Bool) Bool =
          iter.next() %(
            none true
            some(next) check(next.item) and next.rest.all(check))
      code
        any[A](iter: Iter[A], check: fun(A) Bool) Bool =
          iter.next() %(
            none false
            some(next) check(next.item) or next.rest.any(check))
      code
        any(iter: Iter[Bool]) Bool = iter.any(\ value: Bool -> value)
      code
        skip[A](iter: Iter[A], amount: Int) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next().flat_map(\ next: Next[A] ->
              next.rest.skip(amount - 1).next()))
      code
        skip_while[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next() %(
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next)
                if checker(next.item)
                  next.rest.skip_while(checker).next()
                  some(next)))
      code
        take[A](iter: Iter[A], amount: Int) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            if amount == 0
              none[Next[A]]()
              iter.next().map(\ next: Next[A] ->
                Next(item = next.item, rest = next.rest.take(amount - 1))))
      code
        take_while[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next() %(
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next)
                if checker(next.item)
                  some(Next(
                    item = next.item, rest = next.rest.take_while(checker)))
                  none[Next[A]]()))
      code
        # Zips two iters of equal length.
        zip[A, B](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[B]) Iter[Tuple2[A, B]] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            a.next() %(
              none
                _ = assert(b.next() is none, "zip iters have different lengths")
                none[Next[Tuple2[A, B]]]()
              some(aa)
                b.next() %(
                  none crash("zip iters have different lengths")
                  some(bb)
                    some(Next(
                      item = tuple(aa.item, bb.item),
                      rest = zip(aa.rest, bb.rest))))))
      code
        zip_short[A, B](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[B]) Iter[Tuple2[A, B]] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            a.next().flat_map(\ aa: Next[A] ->
              b.next().map(\ bb: Next[B] ->
                Next(
                  item = tuple(aa.item, bb.item),
                  rest = zip_short(aa.rest, bb.rest)))))
      code
        get_maybe[A](iter: Iter[A], index: Int) Maybe[A] =
          iter.skip(index).next().map(\ next: Next[A] -> next.item)
      text
        get[A](iter: Iter[A], index: Int) A =
          iter.get_maybe(index)
            or crash("Index {index.format()} is out of bounds.")
      code
        last[A](iter: Iter[A]) Maybe[A] =
          iter.next().map(\ next: Next[A] -> next.rest.last_rec(next.item))
        last_rec[A](iter: Iter[A], previous: A) A =
          iter.next() %(
            none previous
            some(next) next.rest.last_rec(next.item))
      code
        chain[A](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[A]) Iter[A] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            a.next() %(
              none b.next()
              some(next)
                some(Next(item = next.item, rest = next.rest.chain(b)))))
      code
        flat_map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) Iter[B]) Iter[B] =
          iter_from_fun(\ ->
            iter.next().flat_map(\ next: Next[A] ->
              mapper(next.item).chain(next.rest.flat_map(mapper)).next()))
      code
        flatten[A](iter: Iter[Iter[A]]) Iter[A] =
          iter.flat_map(\ it: Iter[A] -> it)
      code
        cycle[A](iter: Iter[A]) Iter[A] = iter.chain(iter.cycle())
      code
        length[A](iter: Iter[A]) Int =
          iter.fold(0, \ count: Int, item: A -> count + 1)
      code
        sum(iter: Iter[Float]) Float =
          iter.fold(0.0, \ a: Float, b: Float -> a + b)
      code
        sum(iter: Iter[Int]) Int =
          iter.fold(0, \ a: Int, b: Int -> a + b)
      code
        min(iter: Iter[Int]) Maybe[Int] =
          iter.reduce(\ a: Int, b: Int -> min(a, b))
      code
        max(iter: Iter[Int]) Maybe[Int] =
          iter.reduce(\ a: Int, b: Int -> max(a, b))
      code
        iter(range: Range[Int]) Iter[Int] =
          iter_from_state(range.start, \ next: Int ->
            if next == range.end
              none[NextState[Int, Int]]()
              some(NextState(item = next, state = next + 1)))
      code
        iter(range: OpenRange[Int]) Iter[Int] =
          iter_from_state(range.start, \ next: Int ->
            some(NextState(item = next, state = next + 1)))
      code
        iter[T](list: List[T]) Iter[T] =
          iter_from_state(0, \ index: Int ->
            if index == list.length()
              none[NextState[T, Int]]()
              some(NextState(item = list.get(index), state = index + 1)))
        to_list[T](iter: Iter[T]) List[T] =
          iter.fold(list[T](), \ list: List[T], item: T -> list.push(item))
    chapter Hashing
      code
        Hash = struct { value: Int }
      code
        combine(a: Hash, b: Hash) Hash = Hash(value = a.value * 33 + b.value)
        combine_commutative(a: Hash, b: Hash) Hash =
          Hash(value = xor(a.value, b.value))
      code
        hash(int: Int) Hash = Hash(value = int)
      code
        hash(bool: Bool) Hash = {if bool 1 0}.hash()
      code
        hash(byte: Byte) Hash = byte.to_int().hash()
      code
        hash[A](iter: Iter[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash =
          iter.fold(5381.hash(), \ hash: Hash, item: A ->
            combine(hash, hash_item(item)))
      code
        hash_commutative[A](iter: Iter[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash =
          iter.fold(5381.hash(), \ hash: Hash, item: A ->
            combine_commutative(hash, hash_item(item)))
      code
        hash[A](list: List[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash =
          combine(list.length().hash(), list.iter().hash(hash_item))
    chapter Formatting
      code
        format(int: Int) String = int.format(10)
        format(int: Int, radix: Int) String =
          _ = assert(
            radix >= 2 and radix <= 16,
            "Radix must be between 2 and 16.")
          if int < 0
            "-" + format_negative(int, radix)
            format_negative(0 - int, radix)
        # Keep the magnitude negative so min_int never needs to be negated.
        format_negative(int: Int, radix: Int) String =
          rest = int / radix
          digit_value = {rest * radix} - int
          digit = string(list("0123456789abcdef".bytes().get(digit_value)))
          if rest == 0 digit format_negative(rest, radix) + digit
      code
        format(bool: Bool) String = if bool "true" "false"
        format(o: Ordering) String =
          o %(less "less" equal "equal" greater "greater")
  chapter UserInterface
    text
      Orchard supports two-dimensional graphics. The coordinate system starts at
      the top left with (0, 0) and coordinates generally use Floats. All
      graphics are vector-based and use the sRGB color space.
    chapter Color
      text
        Orchard uses the sRGB color space.
      code
        Color = struct { r: Byte, g: Byte, b: Byte }
        color(r: Byte, g: Byte, b: Byte) Color = Color(r, g, b)
      code
        color(hex: String) Color =
          digits = hex.bytes()
          _ = assert(digits.length() == 7, "Expected #RRGGBB")
          _ = assert(digits.get(0) == char("#"), "Expected #RRGGBB")
          color(
            digits.slice(1..3).parse_int(16).to_byte(),
            digits.slice(3..5).parse_int(16).to_byte(),
            digits.slice(5..7).parse_int(16).to_byte())
      text
        format(color: Color) String =
          "#
            '{color.r.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}
            '{color.g.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}
            '{color.b.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}"
    chapter Vec2
      text
        A mathematical two-dimensional vector with no meaning associated to
        the components.
      code
        Vec2[A] = struct { x: A, y: A }
        vec[A](x: A, y: A) Vec2[A] = Vec2(x, y)
      text
        Operations specifically for vectors of floating-point numbers.
      code
        +(a: Vec2[Float], b: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] =
          Vec2(x = a.x + b.x, y = a.y + b.y)
        -(a: Vec2[Float], b: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] =
          Vec2(x = a.x - b.x, y = a.y - b.y)
        *(f: Float, a: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] =
          Vec2(x = f * a.x, y = f * a.y)
        *(a: Vec2[Float], f: Float) Vec2[Float] = f * a
        /(a: Vec2[Float], f: Float) Vec2[Float] =
          Vec2(x = a.x / f, y = a.y / f)
    chapter Offset
      text
        An offset in a two-dimensional plane.
        TODO: Do I need Offset when I have position and size?
      code
        Offset = struct { x: Float, y: Float }
        offset(x: Float, y: Float) Offset = Offset(x, y)
        to_vec(offset: Offset) Vec2[Float] = vec(offset.x, offset.y)
        to_offset(vec: Vec2[Float]) Offset = offset(vec.x, vec.y)
        +(a: Offset, b: Offset) Offset = {a.to_vec() + b.to_vec()}.to_offset()
        -(a: Offset, b: Offset) Offset = {a.to_vec() - b.to_vec()}.to_offset()
        *(f: Float, a: Offset) Offset = {f * a.to_vec()}.to_offset()
        *(a: Offset, f: Float) Offset = {a.to_vec() * f}.to_offset()
        /(a: Offset, f: Float) Offset = {a.to_vec() / f}.to_offset()
    chapter Position
      text
        A position in a two-dimensional plane.
      code
        Position = struct { x: Float, y: Float }
        position(x: Float, y: Float) Position = Position(x, y)
        to_vec(pos: Position) Vec2[Float] = vec(pos.x, pos.y)
        to_position(vec: Vec2[Float]) Position = position(vec.x, vec.y)
        +(pos: Position, offset: Offset) Position =
          {pos.to_vec() + offset.to_vec()}.to_position()
        -(pos: Position, offset: Offset) Position =
          {pos.to_vec() - offset.to_vec()}.to_position()
    chapter Size
      text
        A two-dimensional size.
      code
        Size = struct { width: Float, height: Float }
        size(width: Float, height: Float) Size = Size(width, height)
        to_vec(size: Size) Vec2[Float] = vec(size.width, size.height)
        to_size(vec: Vec2[Float]) Size = size(vec.x, vec.y)
        *(f: Float, size: Size) Size = {f * size.to_vec()}.to_size()
        *(size: Size, f: Float) Size = {size.to_vec() * f}.to_size()
        /(size: Size, f: Float) Size = {size.to_vec() / f}.to_size()
    chapter Rectangle
      code
        Rectangle = struct {
          left: Float, top: Float, right: Float, bottom: Float}
        rectangle(
          left: Float, top: Float, right: Float, bottom: Float
        ) Rectangle =
          Rectangle(left, top, right, bottom)
        rectangle(top_left: Position, size: Size) Rectangle =
          rectangle(
            top_left.x,
            top_left.y,
            top_left.x + size.width,
            top_left.y + size.height)
        top_left(rect: Rectangle) Position = position(rect.left, rect.top)
        top_right(rect: Rectangle) Position = position(rect.right, rect.top)
        bottom_left(rect: Rectangle) Position = position(rect.left, rect.bottom)
        bottom_right(rect: Rectangle) Position =
          position(rect.right, rect.bottom)
        center(rect: Rectangle) Position =
          position(
            {rect.left + rect.right} / 2.0, {rect.top + rect.bottom} / 2.0)
        contains(rect: Rectangle, pos: Position) Bool =
          {rect.left .. rect.right}.contains(pos.x)
            and {rect.top .. rect.bottom}.contains(pos.y)
        intersection(a: Rectangle, b: Rectangle) Maybe[Rectangle] =
          left = max(a.left, b.left)
          top = max(a.top, b.top)
          right = min(a.right, b.right)
          bottom = min(a.bottom, b.bottom)
          if left < right and top < bottom
            some(rectangle(left, top, right, bottom))
            none[Rectangle]()
    chapter Drawings
      text
        When Orchard code is asked to render something for the outside world,
        it returns draw commands. Think of these similar to the elements you
        would find in SVGs.
      chapter HistoricalNote
        text
          Initially, I tried to communicate with the outside world using pixel
          graphics. However, I believe that's not the right move as pixels are
          more difficult to handle in code. Having pixels (or some concept of a
          texture) requires either:
          - mutable values (which Orchard doesn't have), or
          - lots of copying on every texture modification (meh), or
          - a super-smart optimizing compiler (which is useful but it has the
            sort of slow-by-default-and-you-have-to-trust-the-black-box-to-work
            feeling I don't like), or
          - inefficient storage of individual pixels in trees.
          By communicating the intent on a quite high-level, we can instead use
          GPUs when turning the high-level draw commands into pixels.
          This is also similar to how Flutter works: The Dart VM is decently
          fast, but not super fast, so it just emits draw commands that
          the lower-level C++ engine takes and turns into pixels.
      chapter Transformations
        text
          Transformation matrices ... exist. They have this form:
          a b c
          d e f
          TODO: better description
        code
          TransformationMatrix = struct {
            a: Float, b: Float, c: Float, d: Float, e: Float, f: Float}
          transformation_matrix(
            a: Float, b: Float, c: Float, d: Float, e: Float, f: Float
            ) TransformationMatrix =
            TransformationMatrix(a, b, c, d, e, f)
          transform_translate(offset: Offset) TransformationMatrix =
            transformation_matrix(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, offset.x, offset.y)
          transform_scale(factor: Float) TransformationMatrix =
            transformation_matrix(factor, 0.0, 0.0, factor, 0.0, 0.0)
      chapter Paths
        code
          BezierTo = struct { control1: Position, control2: Position, to: Position }
          PathSegment = enum { move_to: Position, line_to: Position, bezier_to: BezierTo }
          Path = struct { segments: List[PathSegment] }
        code
          path() Path = Path(segments = list[PathSegment]())
          move_to(path: Path, pos: Position) Path =
            Path(segments = path.segments.push(PathSegment.move_to(pos)))
          move_to(path: Path, x: Float, y: Float) Path =
            path.move_to(position(x, y))
          line_to(path: Path, pos: Position) Path =
            Path(segments = path.segments.push(PathSegment.line_to(pos)))
          line_to(path: Path, x: Float, y: Float) Path =
            path.line_to(position(x, y))
          bezier_to(
            path: Path, control1: Position, control2: Position, to: Position
            ) Path =
            Path(
              segments =
                path.segments.push(
                  PathSegment.bezier_to(BezierTo(control1, control2, to))))
          bezier_to(
            path: Path,
            c1x: Float, c1y: Float,
            c2x: Float, c2y: Float,
            x: Float, y: Float,
            ) Path =
            path.bezier_to(
              position(c1x, c1y), position(c2x, c2y), position(x, y))
      code
        DrawCommand = enum {
          nothing,
          fill_path: FillPath,
          transform: Transform,
          all: List[DrawCommand],
          clip: Clip}
        FillPath = struct { path: Path, color: Color }
        Transform = struct { by: TransformationMatrix, what: DrawCommand }
        Clip = struct { path: Path, what: DrawCommand }
      code
        draw_nothing() DrawCommand = DrawCommand.nothing
        draw_filled_path(path: Path, color: Color) DrawCommand =
          DrawCommand.fill_path(FillPath(path, color))
        draw_transformed(
          by: TransformationMatrix, what: DrawCommand) DrawCommand =
          DrawCommand.transform(Transform(by, what))
        draw_clipped(path: Path, what: DrawCommand) DrawCommand =
          DrawCommand.clip(Clip(path, what))
        draw_all(commands: List[DrawCommand]) DrawCommand =
          DrawCommand.all(commands)
        draw_colored_rect(size: Size, color: Color) DrawCommand =
          draw_filled_path(
            path()
              .move_to(0.0, 0.0)
              .line_to(size.width, 0.0)
              .line_to(size.width, size.height)
              .line_to(0.0, size.height),
            color)
      code
        Drawing = struct { draw: DrawCommand, size: Size }
    chapter TextRendering
      text
        Layout wraps whole words at spaces and respects explicit newlines.
        Words wider than the available width overflow instead of being split.
        Each line is 13 units high, including a trailing empty line after a
        newline. Empty text has zero size. There is one unit between glyphs.
      code
        Glyph = struct { width: Float, paths: List[Path] }
        pixel_glyph(width: Int, pixels: String) Glyph =
          parsed = pixels.bytes().iter().fold(
            0, 0, list[Path](),
            \ x: Int, y: Int, paths: List[Path], byte: Byte ->
              if byte == char("|")
                tuple(0, y + 1, paths)
                _ = assert(x < width, "Pixel glyph row exceeds its width")
                _ = assert(
                  byte == char(" ") or byte == char("M"),
                  "Invalid glyph pixel")
                px = x.to_float()
                py = y.to_float()
                tuple(
                  x + 1,
                  y,
                  if byte == char(" ")
                    paths
                    paths.push(
                      path()
                        .move_to(px, py)
                        .line_to(px + 1.0, py)
                        .line_to(px + 1.0, py + 1.0)
                        .line_to(px, py + 1.0))))
          _ = assert(parsed.a == 0, "Pixel glyph must end with a row separator")
          Glyph(width = width.to_float(), paths = parsed.c)
      code
        text_line_height = 13.0
        fallback_glyph = pixel_glyph(5,
          "M M M| M M|M M M| M M|M M M| M M|M M M| M M|M M M| M M|")
        # Entries correspond to codepoints 32 through 126, in ASCII order.
        text_glyphs = list[Glyph]()
          .push(pixel_glyph(2, "|"))
          .push(pixel_glyph(2, "|MM|MM|MM|MM|MM|MM||MM|MM|"))
